Define the free_sized fallback normally on Windows.

The runtime provides free_sized for C libraries that predate C23, and
makes it weak so that a real one wins. With GCC on Windows a weak
definition becomes a weak external aliased to the code, and GNU ld cannot
export that from a DLL: linking libInit_shared.dll stops with "cannot
export free_sized: symbol wrong type (2 vs 3)". lld accepts it. Windows'
C runtime has no free_sized for the fallback to give way to, so drop the
weak attribute there. The stage0 snapshot carries the same definition.

Upstream: not submitted
Applies-to: all environments

--- a/src/runtime/object.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/object.cpp
@@ -62,7 +62,10 @@
 // `__attribute__((nothrow))` to be present or `noexcept`.
 __attribute__((nothrow))
 #endif
-__attribute__((weak)) void free_sized(void *ptr, size_t) {
+#ifndef LEAN_WINDOWS
+__attribute__((weak))
+#endif
+void free_sized(void *ptr, size_t) {
     free(ptr);
 }
 #endif
--- a/stage0/src/runtime/object.cpp
+++ b/stage0/src/runtime/object.cpp
@@ -62,7 +62,10 @@
 // `__attribute__((nothrow))` to be present or `noexcept`.
 __attribute__((nothrow))
 #endif
-__attribute__((weak)) void free_sized(void *ptr, size_t) {
+#ifndef LEAN_WINDOWS
+__attribute__((weak))
+#endif
+void free_sized(void *ptr, size_t) {
     free(ptr);
 }
 #endif
